\documentclass PIToolDoc
\tool Finder
\module Finder
\categories { Tricx }
\keywords { search, launcher, process, script, feature script, quick access }
\author { Sven Kopetzki }
\copyright { 2026 Sven Kopetzki }
\brief { Search and launch installed processes and PJSR scripts by name. }

\description {
Finder is a compact search window for all processes and feature scripts installed in PixInsight.
While you type, it lists the processes and scripts whose names contain the search text; \k {Return} or a double click launches the selected one.
This way any process or script is only a few keystrokes away, without searching through the Process and Script menus.

\figure {
\figtag \s {Finder.} Typing a part of a name lists the matching processes and scripts, each with its icon; the first match is selected.
\image images/Finder.png
}

Finder is available as \m {Process > Tricx > Finder}.
Optionally it opens when PixInsight starts and stays in the background as a narrow search line, ready for the next search.
}

\usage {
\subsection { Searching } {
\list {
   { The result list appears as soon as you type into the search field. }
   { Only the names of processes and scripts are searched, ignoring case; the text may be anywhere in the name (\c {stretch} finds \c {AdaptiveStretch} and \c {ArcsinhStretch}). }
   { The list is sorted alphabetically and shows three rows; scroll to see further matches. A tooltip shows the identifier of a process or the full path of a script. }
}
}

\subsection { Keys and mouse } {
\definition {
   { \k {Up}, \k {Down} }
   { Move the selection by one row. }
   { \k {Page Up}, \k {Page Down} }
   { Move the selection by ten rows. }
   { \k {Return} }
   {
Launches the selected process or runs the selected script globally.
The search field is cleared and the result list is hidden; the window stays open in the background unless \lref close_after_launch {Close after launch} is enabled.
   }
   { Double click }
   { Launches the process or script under the mouse pointer, as \k {Return}. }
   { \k {Escape} }
   { Clears the search field. The window is never closed by it. }
}
}

\subsection { Interface buttons } {
\definition {
   { New instance (blue triangle) }
   {
Drag it onto the workspace to create a Finder process icon.
Executing the icon (or double-clicking it) opens the search window; opened this way, the window always closes after launching a process or script.
   }
   { Browse documentation }
   { Opens this document. }
   { Preferences (wrench) }
   { Opens the \lref preferences {preferences} of Finder. }
}
}

\subsection { \label preferences Preferences } {
The preferences are stored with the PixInsight settings and apply to all sessions.

\definition {
   { Open at startup }
   { Opens Finder automatically when PixInsight starts. The window position is restored across sessions. }
   { \label close_after_launch Close after launch }
   {
Closes Finder after launching a process or script with \k {Return}.
Otherwise the window stays open behind the launched process or script (as a narrow search line, if \s {Minimize} is enabled).
   }
   { Minimize }
   {
Shrinks Finder to a narrow search line while it does not have the focus.
Clicking into the search line, or launching Finder again, restores the full window.
Enabled by default.
   }
}
}

\subsection { Indexed scripts } {
Finder lists the \e {feature scripts} of these folders and their subfolders:

\list {
   { The standard script folder of the running PixInsight installation, \c {src/scripts} in its installation folder. }
   { \c {~/PixInsight/Scripts} and \c {~/Documents/PixInsight/Scripts}, if they exist. }
   { Further folders in the environment variable \c {PIXINSIGHT_SCRIPT_PATHS}, separated as in \c {PATH}: by semicolons on Windows, by colons on macOS and Linux. }
}

A JavaScript file (\c {.js}) is listed only if it declares a \c {#feature-id} directive, as PixInsight requires for the Script menu.
Files without it are mostly parts that a main script includes with \c {#include}; run on their own, they fail.
Command scripts (\c {.scp}) are always listed.

A script is shown with the icon of its \c {#feature-icon} directive (SVG, XPM, PNG and other formats); a relative path is resolved from the folder of the script, \c {@script_icons_dir/} from \c {rsc/icons/script} of the installation.
Scripts without a loadable icon get a generic document icon.

\note {
The list is built when Finder is opened for the first time in a session. Scripts installed later appear after a restart of PixInsight.
}
}
}

\related https://tricx.de/pi-scripts/Finder/ { Finder update repository }
\related https://github.com/Trickx/PI-Finder { Finder source code }

\make
